WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:07.180
This episode was made possible by generous
supporters on Patreon.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:08.060
Hey Crazies.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.480
We’ve been doing a lot with space-time diagrams
lately.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.280
Figuring out how different observers measure
the passage of time.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.100
But that got several of you asking:

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.400
Does that mean the future is predetermined and we don’t have freewill?

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.820
Well, maybe, maybe not.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.520
Deep questions like this require careful scrutiny.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:29.060
All we know for sure from diagrams like this
is that time exists.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:33.780
That doesn’t necessarily mean that the future
or even the past exist.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:34.680
Hmm.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.960
Maybe we should define these words first.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.400
The past is the time, or period of time, before
the present.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:46.180
The future is the time, or period of time,
following the present.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:49.180
So both of these are defined in terms of the
current moment.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:50.540
That kind of makes sense.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:53.680
The present, the “now” is what we’re
experiencing.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.060
But it feels like the past exists.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:57.880
We remember the past happening.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:00:59.780
Well, none of us remember that.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:01.180
Or that.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:05.560
But I certainly remember all of these moments,
whether I want to or not.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:09.900
But does that exist? or is that just a
memory we're experiencing now?

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.440
The future also feels like it’s going to
happen

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.380
even if we don’t know what it’ll be.

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:17.720
Does that exist? or is it just a prediction we're making now?

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.160
Hmm.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.600
Let’s see what this looks like on a space-time
diagram.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.620
You are here.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:27.180
This event represents your present. Your now.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:31.980
Whether you’re stationary, moving at a steady
speed, constantly accelerating,

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:36.100
or doing something completely random,
you can’t go any faster than light.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:41.620
Light travels along these diagonal paths,
so your path is stuck inside those lines.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:44.180
This half happened before your present.

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:45.340
It’s your past.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:47.860
All the events that might have influenced
you.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.740
This half will happen after your present.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.020
It’s your future.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:54.600
All the events you might influence.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.440
Your entire life is contained inside these
regions,

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.980
no matter what you might have done or what
you might decide to do.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:04.660
Those two regions are called your past and
future light-cones.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.960
Cones? How are those cones?

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.380
Oh, right right right. Let me explain.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:13.720
Only having a single space axis makes these
look like triangles,

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:18.280
but if you include a second space axis, you
can see they’re really cones.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:21.320
You just have to consider light traveling
in any direction.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.200
Actually, there are three space axes.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.900
Yeah yeah, but our axis limit is three
and one of them has to be time.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.680
Besides, most motion happens in two dimensions
anyway.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:32.540
Let’s not over-complicate this.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.999
Anyway, this still leaves some things open.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.720
I said the word "might" a lot during that
space-time diagram.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.860
So are we predetermined to travel our space-time
paths or not?

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:46.980
This comes down to an idea called causality
or "cause and effect."

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:52.040
And relativity rule number 6 says it must
be maintained, no matter what.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:57.460
That means, if one event causes another, then
everyone agrees they happen in that order.

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:59.480
Cause before effect.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:03.960
Let’s say you are the cause and you want
to affect me at particular time.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.160
I must be somewhere in your future light-cone.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:11.000
Maybe I’m directly on the path you’re
taking, so you’ll be able to touch me.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.980
Maybe I’m not on your path, but I’m close
enough to throw a rock at.

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:19.720
Maybe I’m so far away from you, the only
way you can affect me is with light.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:25.040
In all cases, from all points of view, the
cause comes before the effect.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.060
So is all of time just a string of cause and
effect?

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:29.960
Eh, not so fast.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:35.559
If an event happens outside your influence,
the distinction between past and future kind

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:37.100
of melts away.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:41.660
Say there’s an event outside your future
light-cone, outside your influence.

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:45.839
You might still think it happens in the future
because, on your timeline, it happens at a

00:03:45.839 --> 00:03:47.089
later time.

00:03:47.089 --> 00:03:49.980
But someone else might say it happened in
the past

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:53.560
because, on their timeline, it happened at
an earlier time.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:58.300
Wait wait, something can happen in one person’s
future, but another person’s past?

00:03:58.300 --> 00:03:59.240
Yep.

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:03.040
So the future and the past are very personal
concepts.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:04.680
They belong to an observer.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:08.780
But an observer is only in the present, which
reminds of something.

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:13.020
There’s one property of the universe we
haven’t considered: Locality.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:14.980
or in local-ity.

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:18.280
We’ve discussed this in previous videos,
but it could bear some repeating.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:23.060
Whether we’re talking basic mechanics, electromagnetism, space-time curvature,

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:27.100
or even quantum fields; the universe is inherently local.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:32.340
The idea is that a physical thing can only
be influenced by its immediate surroundings.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:35.480
In other words,
in order for a thing over there to affect

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:39.780
me here, it has to either come here
or send something here.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:41.820
Which brings us to another relevant question:

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:43.600
How big is here?

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.340
It’s really small.

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:49.120
Local for a person might look like this.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:50.580
This is pretty small for me.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:53.040
OK, I have to admit, this is a little uncomfortable.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.140
Anyway, this is my space, my here.

00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:58.410
And it works for anything the size of me.

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:02.380
If that local box is too small, like it would be for my space station,

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:04.960
then it’s a little overkill, but it’s fine.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:07.740
If it’s way too big, like it would be for
particles,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.300
then it simply won’t work.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:10.920
So smaller is better.

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:15.820
Ideally, it would have exactly zero size,
but that poses a few problems.

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:18.800
The main problem being that we can’t divide
by zero.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.160
It just doesn’t work.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.160
Luckily, we’ve got a trick that gets us
around this problem.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:26.640
Let’s start with two points separated by
this much space.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:31.600
Say this point is at 5 and this point is at
6, or 5 plus 1,

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:33.560
because they’re separated by one unit.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:36.080
Maybe it’s inches, maybe it’s centimeters.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:37.740
Whatever. It doesn’t matter.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:42.670
If we make the space a little smaller, maybe the second point might be at 5 plus one half,

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:47.770
or 5 plus one tenth, or 5 plus one hundredth,
or 5 plus one thousandth.

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:49.840
The points keep getting closer together.

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:53.140
They’re almost the same point, but not quite.

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:56.580
We can keep this up, we can get closer
and closer

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:57.760
and closer.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:03.000
Until the points are at 5 and 5 plus an unimaginably
tiny number.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.330
So tiny, it’s not even useful to label it
as a number anymore.

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:11.020
We call it an infinitesimal and label it dx.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:12.620
Welcome to calculus!

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:14.810
I know my calculus.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:18.360
You plus me equals us.

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:24.140
Anyway, an infinitesimal is an amount of space
that is as close to zero as we can get

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:26.580
without actually being zero.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:29.060
It’s as local as our math can get.

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:31.660
Of course, dx is just one dimension.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:35.200
dx and dy make two dimensions.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:40.560
Include a dz and you’ve got three dimensions,
also known as space.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:44.340
Include a dt and you’ve got a four-dimensional
space-time.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:51.100
The volume dx dy dz is the local here and
the dt is the local now.

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:52.880
That’s all that really exists.

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:55.920
There is no universal reference frame because
there is no universe

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:58.880
at least in the way that people usually understand
it.

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:04.360
This space-time diagram is only a map of predictions
about a non-existent future.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:07.180
and predictions about a non-existent past.

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:10.360
It’s attached to you as you move through
space-time.

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:14.620
Space-time doesn’t deny you freewill because
the future doesn’t exist.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:19.320
All that exists for anyone is their local
here and their local now.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:21.860
You might not have control over much in your
life.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:25.760
But you always have a choice about what kind
of person you are here and now.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:27.240
Make that choice a good one.

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:28.940
Please share your thoughts in the comments.

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:30.560
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:33.150
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like
to keep up with us.

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:36.380
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:39.860
Why is the speed of light that specific number?

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:43.960
Well, we know it can’t go at infinite speed,
because that would violate causality.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:45.480
It has to be finite.

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:49.080
But the exact value is kind of an accident, as far as we can tell.

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:52.680
It could just as easily be a different value
in a different universe.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:54.740
That universe just might not have humans in
it.

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:56.340
Anyway, thanks for watching!

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:57.300
Fast Fast!

